Lane D hand-off: carry the stored Gen I/II name bytes to the legality engine

Why: GB::Entity1/Entity2 (include/Legality/GBLegality.h) take the stored trainer-name and
nickname bytes (otRaw/nickRaw). With them GBLegality.cpp checks the in-game-trade marker's
terminator (pokered macros/data.asm dname writes "<TRAINER>@"), warns on a byte the font has
no glyph for (the decoders stop there, so the name reads shorter than it is stored), and
reads a Hangul page byte at the start of the trainer name as Korean Gold/Silver's
(PK1/PK2.Korean, StringConverter2KOR.IsHangul), which on a PK1 excludes every Gen I origin.
Until the save parsers carry the bytes those rules are unreachable from Legality::analyze;
tools/pkhex_gb_diff/production_parity.cpp counts the corpus records whose verdict needs
them. tests/test_gb_legality_names.cpp `production()` documents today's gap and is where
the expectations change once this lands.

Files outside Lane D's ownership (Integration save parsers; the two Legality.cpp hunks
outside the GB hand-off): not applied on lane-d-gb, proposed here for the owning lane.

Notes for the reviewer:
- The Integration PokemonRecord gains two vectors; every existing field is unchanged, so
  callers that copy the record keep working. A Japanese save stores 6-byte fields, an
  international one 11; the engine takes either.
- With the bytes present, "Original trainer name is empty" is left to GB::trainerNameProblem
  (GBLegality.cpp), which measures a Korean name on its bytes. The old check stays for a
  record built without bytes (tests and tools).
- Korean saves are not supported by the parsers; a Korean Pokemon inside a Western save
  (traded on the cartridges) is what the bytes reveal.

Apply from the repository root: patch -p1 < docs/legality/handoff/lane-d-raw-names.patch

